{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1795818100,"nonce":"ZFlDmWprtevVYoNrovsLJAzfNqA=","title":"CSS gap, overflow and border shorthands do not reset their longhands","message":"In CSS a shorthand resets every longhand it covers. Blinc keeps the longhand\nbeside the shorthand and applies it afterwards, so an earlier or lower\ndeclaration wins.\n\n- `row-gap: 4px; column-gap: 5px; gap: 10px` lays out with the axis gaps, in\n  one rule and across two rules.\n- `overflow-x: scroll; overflow: hidden` keeps the horizontal axis scrolling.\n- `border-top: 6px solid red` followed by a rule with `border: 2px solid blue`\n  keeps the red top side.\n\nReproduced with layout tests that apply the stylesheet to a tree. The fix is\nin two places: the property parsers (declarations in one block) and\n`ElementStyle::merge` (across rules), plus any path that applies styles to\nthe same node one after another.","files":null}]}